course: medicine
lesson: 77
title: Olympiad: odd one out, the right order and tall tales ★★★
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: The last lesson of the course is a puzzle hunt with no sums: find the odd one out and say why, put steps and dates in the right order, check a claim, and read what people really mean when they blow things up or play them down.
explain: An odd-one-out puzzle always has a reason, and the reason is the real answer: cold, flu, a viral sore throat and a bacterial chest infection look alike, but only one of them is helped by antibiotics.
explain: Order puzzles test what the course taught step by step: after a head knock the way back goes rest, everyday activity, light exercise, non-contact training, full training and only then matches, and skipping a step is the trap.
explain: Tall tales come in two sizes: «I'm literally dying of thirst» after a match just means thirsty, but «it's just a scratch» about a gaping cut, or «I'm fine, man» from a mate who keeps asking the score, plays down something real, and the wise move is to check and tell an adult.
fact: Antibiotics work on bacteria, not on viruses; colds, flu and most sore throats are caused by viruses.
fact: Germs include bacteria, viruses and fungi; histamine is not a germ but a substance the body releases in an allergy.
fact: Burns are cooled under cool running water for 20 minutes, then covered loosely with cling film; butter, toothpaste and ice are never put on a burn.
fact: Return to sport after concussion goes in stages: rest, everyday activity, light exercise, non-contact training, full training, then matches, and school comes back before sport.
fact: Bone heals in stages: a blood clot, then soft new bone called a callus, which slowly hardens over several weeks.
fact: If someone is in trouble in water: Call 999, Tell them to float, Throw something that floats, and never go in after them.
fact: Edward Jenner made the first vaccine in 1796; Semmelweis showed handwashing saved lives in the 1840s; Pasteur and Koch proved germs cause disease in the 1860s to 1880s; Fleming noticed penicillin in 1928; smallpox was declared wiped out in 1980.
fact: Carrots don't give night vision, the 5-second rule is a myth, dock leaves have no proven medicine in them, and weeing on a jellyfish sting doesn't help.
fact: Tetanus germs live in soil, so the danger in a wound is the dirt, not the rust.
fact: A sore arm or a slight fever for a day or two after a jab is the immune system responding, not the disease.
fact: Scrum caps and helmets help with cuts and bumps but cannot stop concussion.
fact: Heat exhaustion should get better within about 30 minutes of cooling; still unwell after that, confused, or hot but not sweating means heatstroke and 999.
fact: After a missed dose of medicine you never take two to catch up.
fact: «Mardy» in the Midlands and North means sulky; «yeah nah» in Australia and New Zealand means no.
exercise: Odd one out: cold, flu, a viral sore throat, a bacterial chest infection. Which is it and why? | The chest infection: antibiotics can help it | The cold: it's the only one you catch | The flu: it's the only one with a fever | The sore throat: it's the only one in the neck
exercise: Odd one out: bacteria, viruses, fungi, histamine. Which is it? | Histamine, it isn't a germ at all | Fungi, they are much too big to be germs | Bacteria, most of them are helpful | Viruses, they are too small to see
exercise: Odd one out for a burn: butter, toothpaste, ice, cool running water. | Cool running water, the only right one | Ice, because it is the coldest one | Butter, because it is the only food | Toothpaste, the only one from a tube
exercise: Which list is in the right order for a burn? | Cool 20 minutes, then cover loosely | Cover tightly, then cool 20 minutes | Butter first, then cool water | Pop the blister, then cool it
exercise: After a concussion, which step comes straight after light exercise? | Non-contact training | A full competitive match | Full training with tackles | Rest in a dark room
exercise: Which list puts the return after a concussion in the right order? | Rest, everyday activity, light exercise, matches last | Matches, then rest, then light exercise | Light exercise, then rest, then everyday activity | Rest, then matches, then light exercise
exercise: What comes back first after concussion, school or sport? | School | Sport | Both on the same day | Neither for a whole year
exercise: Put the healing of a broken bone in order. | Blood clot, soft callus, hard bone | Hard bone, blood clot, soft callus | Soft callus, blood clot, hard bone | Hard bone, soft callus, blood clot
exercise: Which list gives the water rescue rule in the right order? | Call, Tell, Throw | Throw, Call, Tell | Jump in, Call, Tell | Tell, Jump in, Throw
exercise: Which of these happened first? | Jenner's first vaccine | Fleming and penicillin | Semmelweis and handwashing | Smallpox wiped out
exercise: Which of these happened last? | Smallpox declared wiped out | Jenner's first vaccine | Fleming noticing penicillin | Pasteur and Koch proving germ theory
exercise: Claim check: «a rusty nail is dangerous because of the rust». What's really true? | The soil and dirt carry tetanus germs | Rust itself is a germ that grows | Only shiny nails can make you ill | Rust turns into a poison in blood
exercise: Odd one out: carrots for night vision, the 5-second rule, dock leaves cure nettle stings, washing hands for 20 seconds. | Washing hands, the only one that's true | Carrots, the only one that's a food | Dock leaves, the only one that's a plant | The 5-second rule, the only one about time
exercise: «Sore arm and a bit hot after my jab, so it gave me the flu». What's wrong with that? | It's the immune system responding | Nothing, jabs do give you the disease | The nurse used the wrong arm | Jabs only make you hot if you run
exercise: Which kit cannot stop concussion? | A helmet or a scrum cap | Shin pads on the shins | A mouth guard for teeth | Boots with good studs
exercise: Ben forgot his morning dose and remembers at teatime, close to the next one. What does he never do? | Take two at once to catch up | Ask an adult what to do now | Read the leaflet with an adult | Write down the time he took it
test: «I've told you a MILLION times, butter on a burn» says Nan. How often has she told it, and is it right? | Lots of times, and it's still wrong | A million times, so it must be right | Once, and it's right | Never, she's joking
test: After the match Callum: «literally dying of thirst, mate». What does he really mean, and what helps? | He's very thirsty, so water | He's in danger, so call 999 | He's fine, so nothing | He's hungry, so crisps
test: Rhys says «it's just a scratch» about a gaping cut on his knee. What's the real size of the problem? | Big enough that it may need stitches | Tiny, a plaster and it's all forgotten | Nothing at all, scratches heal on their own | Small, but he should pick the scab later
test: Freya, after a head clash, asks the score for the third time: «I'm fine, man, howay». What's going on? | She may have concussion, she must come off | She's just bored and wants to know | She's fine, she says so herself | She's tired and needs a sports drink
test: «It's a bit warm out» says Dad at sports day, and it's the hottest day of the year. Is he blowing it up or playing it down? | Playing it down | Blowing it up, it's only mild | Neither, it's exactly warm | Blowing it up, it's actually cold
test: Leah has been cooled in the shade for 30 minutes, is still dizzy and now confused. Heat exhaustion or something worse? | Worse: heatstroke, an adult calls 999 | Heat exhaustion, just give her a cola | Nothing, she's just a bit mardy | Worse, so she should run it off
test: «yeah nah, I'm not jumping in after the dog» says Mia at the frozen pond. What does she mean, and is she right? | No, and she's right to stay out | Yes, and she's right to go in | Yes, but she should wait an hour | No, but she's wrong to stay out
test: Jake's mate: «my cousin's a doctor and says antibiotics fix colds, no cap». How do you check that claim? | Colds are viruses, antibiotics only hit bacteria | Ask how old his cousin is first | Colds are bacteria, so he's right | Antibiotics fix everything with a fever
test: Odd one out with a reason: 999 in the UK, 911 in the USA, 000 in Australia, 111 in the UK. | 111 in the UK, it's not for emergencies | 911, it's the only one in America | 000, it's the only one with no 9 | 999, it's the only one with three 9s
test: Which history list is in order, earliest first? | Jenner, Semmelweis, Fleming | Fleming, Jenner, Semmelweis | Semmelweis, Fleming, Jenner | Jenner, Fleming, Semmelweis
test: «That rip didn't half pull, I was nearly in France!» says Tom off a Devon beach. What does he really mean? | The current pulled him out a long way | He swam all the way across to France | The rip pulled him under the water | The water was nice and warm today
exam: Identify the item which does not belong to the group, with the reason: bacteria, viruses, fungi, histamine. | Histamine, as it is not a germ | Fungi, as they cause no illness | Viruses, as they are the largest | Bacteria, as they are all useful
exam: State the correct order of the first three stages of return to sport following concussion. | Rest, everyday activity, light exercise | Light exercise, rest, everyday activity | Everyday activity, matches, rest | Rest, matches, full training
exam: Determine which of the following was declared wiped out in 1980 following a worldwide vaccination campaign. | Smallpox | Measles | Polio | Chickenpox
exam: Assess the claim that weeing upon a jellyfish sting relieves the pain. | It is a myth | It is true and widely advised | It is true only in cold seas | It is true for children only
